<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The bundle's property list, templated the way `debian/control.in` and
  `windows/AppxManifest.xml.in` are. `build-app.sh` substitutes the placeholders;
  nothing here is meant to be edited per build.

  ONE TEMPLATE, THREE BUNDLES

  odox ships three applications and therefore three bundles and three store
  records. One template substituted three ways rather than three files, for the
  reason the package manifest gives: three files describing three formats in
  three near-identical ways is three chances for one to drift with nothing to
  notice.

  The launcher gets no bundle. It hands off by replacing itself with the right
  viewer, which macOS has no use for, and it ships only on apt.

  TWO KINDS OF DECLARATION, WHICH ARE NOT THE SAME THING

  `UTImportedTypeDeclarations` says what an OpenDocument file *is*.
  `CFBundleDocumentTypes` says that this application handles it. A bundle
  carrying only the second claims documents it never described; one carrying only
  the first describes types nothing opens.

  The declarations are **imported**, and the identifiers are OASIS's rather than
  invented. This is where the bundle differs from the rest of the fleet's: segler
  had to mint `com.excelano.*` identifiers because DocLang names no UTI, and
  slipcase-desktop exports a type it owns. OpenDocument has registered
  identifiers, `org.oasis-open.opendocument.*`, and macOS has declared them for
  years, so importing them is both correct and the thing that makes these
  applications agree with everything else on the machine. An imported declaration
  yields to the system's own, which is the posture a reader of somebody else's
  format should have.

  `LSHandlerRank` is **Alternate** rather than Default or Owner. These read a
  format this project does not own, on a machine where a full office suite may
  also claim it, and Alternate is the value that says *I can open this* without
  asking to be preferred. A person can still choose it in Get Info.

  `CFBundleTypeRole` is **Viewer**, because these read and do not write. Editor
  would be a claim to the platform that is not true, and it is the claim that
  changes the day the editing branch lands rather than a value to set early.

  WHAT THE UPLOAD REFUSES WITHOUT

  `LSApplicationCategoryType` is required by App Store Connect and refused at
  upload without one. Productivity is what the `.desktop` entries say with
  `Office;Viewer;`.

  `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption` false is answered here so that it is not asked
  once per upload. These applications contain no encryption: a package is
  inflated and its XML parsed, and an encrypted OpenDocument package is refused
  rather than decrypted.

  `CFBundleVersion` is the commit count and not the release version, because App
  Store Connect refuses a second upload carrying a build number it has seen,
  including a rejected one resubmitted unchanged. `packaging/version.sh` prints
  both spellings.

  `LSMinimumSystemVersion` is 11.0. Nothing here calls anything newer than the
  Apple Event manager, and 11.0 is where Apple silicon begins, so an arm64 slice
  cannot claim lower whatever the floor says. The executable has to agree:
  `build-app.sh` refuses a slice built for a different floor, and
  `MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=11.0` is what sets it.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>

	<!--
	  The languages the bundle claims, which is a separate thing from the
	  languages the window speaks. `crates/odox-ui/po/de.po` is the window's
	  words; this is the package's claim about them, and translating the
	  application does not reach it.

	  It bites twice if it is missing. App Store Connect lists English alone,
	  and macOS builds its per-application language picker in System Settings
	  from this key, so nobody can set one of these to German on an English
	  machine. The Windows side never had the hole, because the package
	  manifest declares `en-us` and `de-de` and `makepri` joins them, which is
	  how it stayed hidden: only the Mac bundle was silent.

	  The array matches `odox_ui::i18n::LANGUAGES` in a release build: English,
	  which is the source language, and German. The pseudolocale beside them is
	  `cfg(debug_assertions)` and never ships.

	  Found by the Duckling session on 2026-09-14, against its own bundle, and
	  neither segler nor slipcase-desktop had it either. A fleet miss rather
	  than anything this repository dropped.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>de</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>@EXECUTABLE@</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>@IDENTIFIER@</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>

	<!--
	  The product name rather than the binary's, which is the store record's
	  name and what a person sees in the Dock, the menu bar and Finder. The
	  binary stays lowercase; DESIGN.md 9 has the naming rule and the fleet
	  makes the same split.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>@PRODUCT@</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>@PRODUCT@</string>

	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>@VERSION@</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>@BUILD@</string>

	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>@ICON@</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.productivity</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>11.0</string>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>@TYPE_NAME@</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Alternate</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>@UTI@</string>
			</array>
			<key>CFBundleTypeIconFile</key>
			<string>@DOCUMENT_ICON@</string>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Conformance is the macOS half of what `sub-class-of` does in a shared MIME
	  database, and it is true of every OpenDocument file: the package is a ZIP,
	  so an archive tool has something to offer. `public.data` is stated although
	  `public.zip-archive` already conforms to it, so a reader does not have to
	  walk Apple's conformance tree to learn that a document is a file of bytes.

	  There is no magic-bytes tag. A ZIP signature identifies every OpenDocument
	  package alike and every other ZIP besides; what distinguishes one is the
	  `mimetype` entry inside it, which no tag specification can express and
	  which `odox_core::Package` reads directly.
	-->
	<key>UTImportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>@UTI@</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>@TYPE_NAME@</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.zip-archive</string>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeIconFile</key>
			<string>@DOCUMENT_ICON@</string>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>@EXTENSION@</string>
				</array>
				<key>public.mime-type</key>
				<array>
					<string>@CONTENT_TYPE@</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
